[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 12:47] Okay. Your own sizing assumes no volume loss, and the data says nothing about how customers respond to price. If a 10% increase did cost you transactions, how would that change your recommendation?

[candidate · 14:17] It would depend on how much volume we lost. Rough logic: at 10% higher prices, if volume fell a lot, revenue could end up flat or down, and COGS is variable so it falls with volume too — but labor and overhead are partly fixed, so those would get worse as a percent of revenue.

Quick sense of it: gross margin today is only about 42 cents on the dollar after COGS, so we don't have much cushion. If we lost, say, 10% of transactions, we'd roughly break even on revenue and the fixed costs would still hurt us.

So I'd soften the recommendation — pilot it in a few markets rather than rolling out chain-wide, and start supplier renegotiation at the same time rather than treating it as the slower backup.
